<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gioachino Rossini</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Gioachino Antonio Rossini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gioachino Rossini was an Italian composer and conductor of the late Classical and early Romantic eras, best known for his 39 operas. Born in Pesaro in 1792 to musician parents, he saw his first opera performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1810, and between 1810 and 1823 he wrote 34 operas for the Italian stage, among them the comic operas L'italiana in Algeri, The Barber of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and La Cenerentola and the serious Tancredi, Otello and Semiramide. Contracted by the Opéra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1824, he wrote his last opera, Guillaume Tell, in 1829, and then withdrew from opera for the last 40 years of his life, for reasons never fully explained. He died at Passy in 1868.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gioachino-rossini.jpg" alt="Gioachino Rossini" width="300" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: uncredited · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rossini_young-circa-1815.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gioachino Antonio Rossini was born on 29 February 1792 in Pesaro, then part of the Papal States, the only child of Giuseppe Rossini, a trumpeter and horn player, and Anna, née Guidarini, a seamstress. His father was imprisoned at least twice, in 1790 for insubordination and in 1799 and 1800 for republican activism and support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s troops. In 1798 his mother began a career as a singer in comic opera. By twelve Rossini had composed a set of string sonatas, his first surviving work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His first staged opera, the one-act comedy La cambiale di matrimonio, was given at the Teatro San Moisè in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in November 1810. In 1815 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> to write operas and manage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>. Tancredi and L'italiana in Algeri, premiered in Venice on 6 February and 22 May 1813, set his reputation. He wrote 27 operas between 1812 and 1819, often at short notice, and had little over three weeks to write La Cenerentola in 1817. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1822 audiences greeted him with what biographers call Rossini fever.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> he wrote Il viaggio a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> for the coronation of Charles X in June 1825 and reused about half its score in Le comte Ory (1828). Guillaume Tell followed in 1829, running three hours and forty-five minutes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Guillaume Tell he wrote little. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830 cut his lifetime annuity. Ill health kept him from large works; he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1836, lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>, and returned to Paris in 1855, where from December 1858 he held Saturday evening salons attended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-liszt">Franz Liszt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Giuseppe Verdi</a>. His last major composition was the Petite messe solennelle (1863).</p><p>After his death on 13 November 1868, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a> initiated the Messa per Rossini, written with a dozen other composers. Liszt and others made piano transcriptions of his melodies, and Italian opera stayed indebted to his innovations until verismo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gioachino Rossini” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gioachino Rossini</b> was house composer and artistic director of the royal opera houses from 1815 to 1822 and wrote ten operas there; Domenico Barbaja's offer brought Gaetano Donizetti to Naples until 1844, where 51 of his operas were presented, and Vincenzo Bellini's first professionally staged opera opened at…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
